const SOURCE_NOTES_01_THE_PIPELINE_MD = `# 1. The pipeline

> Source note for the narrative, §1, and deck slides 1–2. The stages are the ones in runbooks/feature-pipeline.md; correct them there first.

## What it is
Since week 32, a Linear issue labelled \`ready-for-build\` becomes pull requests without anyone writing code until review. Six bots hand the work along with events, and each one keeps its own thread:

| Stage | Bot | Hands on |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Spec | intake | \`feature.spec\` with \`/specs/<ISSUE>-<slug>.md\` |
| Plan | planner | one \`task.ready\` per task, from \`/specs/<ISSUE>-plan.md\` |
| Build | implementer | one sandbox and one pull request per task; \`pr.opened\` |
| CI | ci-watch | a failure on a task branch, steered into the running task |
| Review | pr-reviewer | \`review.approved\`, and \`release.note\` for customer-visible changes |
| Notes | release-scribe | the Unreleased section of CHANGELOG.md |

## Where people stay in the loop
- **People merge.** Nothing in the pipeline pushes to main.
- **People approve production.** Octopus Deploy waits for a second person, who has read the notes and can roll back.
- **People own the spec.** The spec is in the \`specs\` workspace; editing it and replaying \`task.ready\` rebuilds a task.

**One line for the title slide:** One issue in, reviewed pull requests out.
`;

const SOURCE_NOTES_02_ONE_ISSUE_END_TO_END_MD = `# 2. One issue, end to end

> Source note for the narrative, §2, and deck slide 3. Taken from /specs/LIN-1421-rate-limits.md, /specs/LIN-1421-plan.md and the bots' threads; times are minutes after the label went on.

## LIN-1421: per-API-key rate limits
Marco asked for it: one noisy integration should not starve the others in the same org. Over the limit, \`/api\` answers \`429\` with \`Retry-After\`.

## What happened
- **Minute 0:** the \`ready-for-build\` label fires the Linear webhook.
- **Minute 2:** intake writes the spec. It found the unmounted limiter from #472 and the refill bug that #482 shipped and reverted, and it wrote both into the spec's context.
- **Minute 7:** planner splits the work into three independent tasks: a, the token bucket; b, the middleware and configuration; c, the metrics and customer docs.
- **Minutes 8–80:** implementer builds each task in its own sandbox, with repo-explorer and test-writer as sub-agents, and opens **#491**, **#492** and **#493**.
- **Along the way:** a lint failure on #492's branch is steered into the running task by ci-watch, and fixed in the same pull request.
- **Review:** #491 and #492 are approved. #493's review hit a provider error (529, overloaded) and is waiting to be replayed.
- **Notes:** release-scribe adds the 429 entry to Unreleased in CHANGELOG.md.

## What it could not do alone
The spec has one open question: does the limit apply to \`/api\` only, or to every authenticated route? Intake could not post it to the issue, because Linear returned 401 after the workspace key was rotated. **The assumption is \`/api\` only** until someone answers.

---
Sources: specs workspace; the intake, planner, implementer, ci-watch, pr-reviewer and release-scribe threads.
`;

const SOURCE_NOTES_03_DELIVERY_MD = `# 3. Delivery

> Source note for the narrative, §3, and deck slides 4–5 (charts: Images/Lead Time per Feature.png, Images/Pull Requests by Origin.png). Lead time is from the \`ready-for-build\` label to the last pull request open, from Linear and GitHub.

## Lead time per feature
The last four features built by hand against the four through the pipeline. Lead time stops at "pull requests open", because review and merge are still done by people and take as long as they did.

| Feature | Built | Hours to PRs open |
| --- | --- | --- |
| LIN-1388 billing ledger | by hand | 62 |
| LIN-1395 cookie sessions | by hand | 41 |
| LIN-1402 Retry-After header | by hand | 38 |
| LIN-1405 bounded scheduler retries | by hand | 55 |
| LIN-1411 idempotent billing webhooks | pipeline | 7.5 |
| LIN-1414 version on /healthz | pipeline | 4.2 |
| LIN-1418 session refresh hint | pipeline | 2.1 |
| LIN-1421 per-API-key rate limits | pipeline | 1.3 |

The median went from **48 hours to 3.2 hours**. The early pipeline runs were slower because the specs were still in the wiki, which the bots could not read. Moving them into the \`specs\` workspace fixed that.

## Who opens the pull requests
Merged pull requests a week, by who opened them:

| Week | 29 | 30 | 31 | 32 | 33 | 34 | 35 | 36 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| People | 14 | 12 | 15 | 11 | 13 | 12 | 10 | 12 |
| Pipeline | 0 | 0 | 0 | 2 | 4 | 6 | 7 | 9 |

People did not open fewer pull requests. The pipeline's work is on top, and review is now the bottleneck: the median time from open to merge went from 5 hours to 9.

---
Sources: Linear (label history), GitHub (pull requests merged to main, by author).
`;

const SOURCE_NOTES_04_CI_AND_RELEASES_MD = `# 4. CI and releases

> Source note for the narrative, §4, and deck slides 6–7 (chart: Images/CI Job Durations.png). Timings are medians of ci.yml runs on main, from GitHub Actions.

## The slow week
CI felt slow all week. The status board was down (502), so the timings came from the workflow runs instead. Every job was flat except \`install\`:

| Job | Last week | Slow week | After #486 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| install | 41 s | 2 m 58 s | 44 s |
| lint | 48 s | 51 s | 49 s |
| test (node 22) | 3 m 10 s | 3 m 22 s | 3 m 08 s |
| build image | 2 m 05 s | 2 m 11 s | 2 m 06 s |

**Cause:** #483 bumped \`actions/setup-node\` to v5 and, in the same edit, dropped \`cache: npm\`, so every run did a cold \`npm ci\`. **Fix:** #486 restored the cache, taking about 2 minutes off every run.

## The scheduler flake
The scheduler retry test failed about one run in five, because it depended on the wall clock. #487 makes it use fake timers. The audit that followed found no other wall-clock dependencies in \`src/scheduler\`.

## v2.14.1
Both fixes ship in v2.14.1. The deploy checklist passed: staging soaked for four hours, with no new Sentry issues and a flat dead-letter count. Promotion to production is waiting for the second approval in Octopus Deploy, as the runbook asks.

---
Sources: GitHub Actions (ci.yml runs on main); acme-web/CHANGELOG.md; runbooks/deploy-checklist.md.
`;

const SOURCE_NOTES_05_NEXT_QUARTER_MD = `# 5. Next quarter

> Source note for the narrative, §5, and deck slide 8. From the team's retro; the asks are Priya's.

## What changes
- **Review is the bottleneck.** Pull requests from the pipeline wait 9 hours for a person (median). Each squad gets a daily review slot, and pr-reviewer's approval goes first so people start from its notes.
- **Answer the spec's questions before building.** LIN-1421 was built on an assumption, because the open question could not reach the issue. Intake will hold the build when a spec has an open question that changes the interface.
- **Credentials rotate without breaking the pipeline.** The Linear key rotation silently stopped intake from commenting. Rotations go on the deploy checklist, with the MCP servers page as the step that reconnects.

## Follow-ups from LIN-1421
- Delete the old limiter, \`src/api/rate-limit.ts\` (#472), now that the bucket has landed.
- Per-key overrides in the admin UI. This was a non-goal for LIN-1421; the configuration shape already allows it.

## Asks
1. One reviewer from each squad on the daily slot, starting week 38.
2. Someone with Linear admin to reconnect the workspace key this week.
3. Agreement that "PRs open" is the lead-time number we track, with review time beside it.
`;

